      "text": "|-6 + n| = 2 - n\nBecause $-2$ and $2$ are on opposite sides of $0$ on the number line, and the absolute value function is always non-negative,\nwe know that the midpoint is $\\dfrac{2 + (-6)}{2} = -2$.\nAnything greater than $-2$ goes up to $0$, and anything less than $-2$ goes down to $0$.\nOnly the right-hand side of the equality, $2 - n$, is less than $0$ \\[[x < 0]\\]\nThis means that both $n + 6$ and $2 - n$ are on the negative numbers to the left of $-2$.\nTherefore, $n + 6 = n - 8$, so $n = 2$.\nWe can check that this satisfies the original equation.\nIs this reasoning and explanation correct? Explain how.\n- No\n- Yes\nto solve the equation $|n + 6| = 2 - n$.\n\nYour approach is correct. Here is the revised solution with detailed reasoning:\n\n[ ]\n|n + 6| = 2 – n\nbecause the absolute value of a number is non-negative and the absolute value of a negative number is equal to the negative of the number, we have two cases:\n\n**Case 1:**\nIf $n + 6 \\geq 0$ or $n \\geq -6$:\n$|n + 6| = n + 6$\nTherefore,\n$n + 6 = 2 - n$\n$2n = -4$\n$n = -2$\n\nThis value of $n$ satisfies the condition $n \\geq -6$.\n\n**Case 2:**\nIf $n + 6 < 0$ or $n < -6$:\n$|n + 6| = -(n + 6)$\nTherefore,\n$-(n + 6) = 2 - n$\n$-n - 6 = 2 - n$\n$-6 = 2$\nThis equation is false. Therefore, there is no solution from this case.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $n = -2$.\n\nI hope this clarifies the reasoning behind the solution. Let me know if you have any further questions!\n\n\n\nSolve the following given the equation: $|5m - 5| = 6 - 5m$.\nIn solving this equation, we first need to rearrange the equation:\n\n\\begin{align}\n|5m - 5|&= 6 - 5m \\\\\n\\end{align}\n\nsince $5-m > m \\forall m$.\n\nNow that we have the equation in the form where we can see the inverse, we must consider the unit vectors. The equation is either true when adding or when subtracting. If we consider the case, when subtracting, we will get\n\n\\begin{align}\n  |5m - 5| &\\geq \\left| 6  - 4.5m \\right| \\\\\n  5m - 5 &\\geq 6 - 4.5 m \\\\\n  9.5 m &\\geq 11 \\\\\n  m &\\geq \\frac{22}{19}\n\\end{align}\n\n\nTherefore, there are infinite solutions between $( \\frac{22}{19},\\infty)$. Let $(a,b)$ be the input, and $(c,d)$ the output. Then, we have\n\n\\begin{align}\n|5a - 5| &= 6 - 5a \\\\\n|5b - 5| &= 6 - 5b\n\\implies\n\\left|\n\\begin{matrix}\n\\frac{a}{5} & 1\n \\\\\n\\frac{b}{5} & 1\n\\end{matrix}\n\\right| &= 0\n\\implies \n\\begin{matrix}\n\\frac{b}{5} - \\frac{a}{5} = 0 \\implies b= \\frac{a}{5}.\n\\end{matrix}\n\\end{align}",
