      "text": "We have two cases to consider:\n\n1. When $n + 6$ is non-negative:\n   \\begin{align*}\n   n + 6 &= 2 - n \\\\\n   2n &= -4 \\\\\n   n &= -2\n   \\end{align*}\n   Since $-2 + 6 = 4$, and $4 \\leq 2 - (-2)$, this solution is valid.\n\n2. When $n + 6$ is negative:\n   \\begin{align*}\n   n + 6 &= 2 - n \\\\\n   2n &= -4 \\\\\n   n &= -2\n   \\end{align*}\n   However, this solution is not valid because it contradicts our assumption that $n + 6 < 0$ in this case.\n\nTherefore, the only valid solution is $\\boxed{-2}$.",
